My work spans GPU design verification, computer architecture, performance analysis, and Internet of Things (IoT) systems. I bring experience in Intel GPU pre-silicon validation, machine learning for GPU performance prediction, and HPC performance monitoring.

== Education
- *New Mexico State University*, /Las Cruces, NM/ \n
PhD in Electrical and Computer Engineering (August 2026 – Present)
- *New Mexico State University*, /Las Cruces, NM/ \n
Master of Science in Electrical Engineering (August 2021 – May 2023) \n
Technical report: [https://tinyurl.com/cpibreakdown Utilizing CPI Breakdown for Bottleneck Analysis of Power9 Processor Performance]
- *Bangladesh University of Engineering and Technology (BUET)*, /Dhaka, Bangladesh/ \n
Bachelor of Science in Electrical and Electronic Engineering (July 2014 – April 2019) \n
Major: Communication Engineering \n
Thesis: [https://tinyurl.com/y2n2qenu A Secured Offline Online Approach for Internet of Things (IoT) Using Real-time Database]

== Experience
- *Intel Corporation*, /Folsom, California/ \n
*GPU Logic Design Engineer* (July 2023 – September 2026)
-- Conducted functional pre-silicon verification for discrete, integrated, and HPC GPUs, focusing on 3D render and compute pipelines.
-- Owned end-to-end validation of Intel Xe-Core ray tracing and texture sampling features, including test planning, test development, and coverage closure.
-- Used UVM methodologies and debugged regression failures in simulation and emulation, collaborating with design and architecture teams.
- *New Mexico State University, Department of ET and SE*, /Las Cruces, NM/ \n
*Graduate Assistant* (January 2023 – May 2023)
-- Instructed Communication Systems I (ET 314) theory and lab sessions and mentored students on assignments and projects.
- *Intel Corporation*, /Folsom, California/ \n
*Graduate Technical Intern* (May 2022 – December 2022)
-- Addressed power flow analysis (ptpx) issues for low-power Intel Xe-Core Sampler designs.
-- Managed regressions for the Meteor Lake Sampler CLT out-of-order feature and identified two silicon hangs.
-- Developed UVM testbenches and worked with gnr, elab, and runsim tools.
- *New Mexico State University, Department of ECE*, /Las Cruces, NM/ \n
*Graduate Research and Teaching Assistant* (August 2021 – May 2022)
-- Applied machine learning to basic block count prediction with NVBit-supported PPT-GPU for PolyBench, Rodinia, Pannotia, and LULESH workloads.
-- Collaborated with Los Alamos National Laboratory on reuse distance and memory tracing toolkits for PPT-GPU, and with Sandia National Laboratories on LDMS sampling for IBM Power9.
-- Taught electronics, digital circuit design, and VHDL to more than 50 students.
- *BRAC University, Department of CSE*, /Dhaka, Bangladesh/ \n
*Adjunct Faculty* (February 2021 – September 2021)
-- Taught VLSI Design (CSE 460) and Digital Electronics and Pulse Techniques (CSE 350), including labs with Proteus, Quartus II, Microwind, and ModelSim.
-- Worked on IoT applications for healthcare and agriculture under Dr. Farhad Hossain at BUET.
- *Nelsite Inc. Ltd.*, /Fukuoka, Japan/ \n
*Semiconductor Engineer* (November 2019 – April 2020)
-- Developed embedded systems with 32-bit ARM Cortex-M4 microcontrollers, Keil, and embedded C.
-- Received on-the-job training in semiconductor fabrication and material characterization.

== Research Interests
GPU architecture and verification, high-performance computing, performance modeling and bottleneck analysis, machine learning, and embedded IoT systems.

